# Matching-to-source example, 0.1.0

The existing common packet can trace a checked matching endpoint to its original detector row
and the selected world's reference record. This bounded replay demonstrates those joins on
the existing two-world synthetic admission fixture. It adds no Engine interface or admission
rule. It is an engineering example, not a general validator or participant exercise.

Select the expected preparation and source digests before consulting results. First run the
existing reviewed-operands source reconstruction and decision certificate verification. For
an enumerated finite anchor, use this procedure:

1. Pair the certificate assignment with `comparison.model.variables`. Select its one
   `renamings.world_encodings` world. Keep that assignment across every anchor and configuration.
2. Use `renamings.anchors` to translate the selected common anchor. Select an `object_mapping`
   row by **world, original anchor and graph ID**. Match its object ID, source record digest and
   proposal members to `admission.reference.worlds[].anchors[].objects[]` in that same world.
3. Follow every member into the admission's proposal registry and original review record.
   Preserve its phase, locator and capture identity. Use the bound observation custody and
   package seal to verify the delivered asset against the original capture bytes.
4. In the selected anchor's detection translations, follow the neutral endpoint to its original
   normalization record. Use that record's source digest, sample token and zero-based
   `source_index` to retrieve the original prediction array row. The displayed row suffix is
   not a substitute for the retained source index.

The replay checks all ten certificate matching pairs, four original prediction arrays and two
original image captures. It rejects graph-only, world-only and anchor-only incomplete joins,
and a source digest substituted from the other world. The eight nonretained rows in the four
source arrays cannot substitute for the selected normalized rows. These controls check the
actual source associations, including cases whose geometry and aggregate losses agree.

The fixture's `shared:o0` names four distinct reference record digests. Full context makes each
lookup unique. Its two source images are byte-identical to the corresponding delivered assets.
The proposals supply only whole-capture locators: point indices and pixel selections remain
unavailable. Each capture precedes the declared reference position by two seconds. A source
link establishes neither motion reconstruction nor a physical correspondence.

The common folder contains the complete private review basis. Both analysts receive identical
bytes only at their authorized later stage. This procedure grants no release or access to a
discovery reviewer. Proposed mapping rows can also survive an open-reference fallback; they
are not evidence that a finite graph or matching certificate exists. Count-only, blocked,
inconsistent and open-reference cases require their own explicit status, not an invented match.

Run from an unchanged selected checkout and the pinned runtime, without Python optimization:

```text
python -B research/perception-matching-trace/0.1.0/replay.py \
  --inputs /absolute/SELECTED_INPUTS.json --inputs-sha256 EXPECTED_INPUT_SELECTION_SHA256 \
  --common /absolute/reviewed --common-sha256 EXPECTED_PREPARATION_SHA256 \
  --decision /absolute/decision.json --decision-sha256 EXPECTED_DECISION_SHA256
```

The retained private checkpoint supplies the exact command, original synthetic fixture bytes,
input selection and capture receipts. `SELECTED_INPUTS.json` contains the existing
reviewed-operands keyword arguments: independently selected binding request, admission request,
admission report and assistance preparation. It is an example replay input, not a new common
operand schema. Its fixture was made with `tests.test_perception_reviewed_operands.prepared_inputs`;
no independent human records were created. Regenerating that fixture selects new paths and
local sealing times, so its digests must be selected afresh.

Stdout is the deterministic trace for those exact selected inputs; stderr contains measured
stage times. Source verification uses the existing common/admission, strict source reader,
normalization and decision checker. The trace is separately structured as joins and direct
source-row comparisons; it is not an independent reimplementation of those trusted components.
The existing coordinate-based matching comparator remains retained engineering evidence.

See [the checkpoint](../../../docs/PERCEPTION_MATCHING_TRACE_2026-09-13.md) and
[verification](verification.json) for exact identities, costs, retained tests and limitations.
Real bounds remain [-8,8]. Actual participant viewing, independent human reference judgments
and external scientific review remain missing; Gate A is unaccepted.
